module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Flatness.BlowingUp.FiniteTypeIdealSheaf
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.QuasiCoherent.FiniteTypeIdealSheaf
import StacksCore.Tactic.EgaSgaAttribute

/-!
# Operations on finite type quasi-coherent sheaves of ideals

A quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals `I` on a scheme is of finite type when its ideal on every
affine open is finitely generated; this is
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.IsFiniteType`, and it is the condition the index poset
of [Stacks, Tag 05JS] imposes on its second component. This file collects the operations on
that condition which the index poset needs, and ends with the directedness statement itself.

The two lattice extremes are immediate, and the supremum of two finite type sheaves of ideals
is of finite type (`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.IsFiniteType.sup`); so is the
inverse image along an *arbitrary* morphism of schemes
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.IsFiniteType.comap`). Those two together are the
directedness of the poset: given finite type `I` on `X`, `I'` on `Y` and any pair of morphisms
`g : Z ⟶ X`, `g' : Z ⟶ Y`, the sheaf of ideals `I.comap g ⊔ I'.comap g'` on `Z` is of finite
type and dominates both in the order `I ≤ J.map g` supplied by the Galois connection
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.map_gc`.

The direct image is the one operation that does **not** preserve finite type along a general
affine morphism: `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.map_bot` identifies `map ⊥ f` with
`f.ker`, and the kernel of an affine morphism need not be of finite type — for `f = Spec` of a
surjection `R ↠ R/J` with `J` not finitely generated it is `J` itself. What is true, and proved
here, is the statement for a closed immersion whose own kernel is of finite type: then the
preimage of a finitely generated ideal under the surjection on sections is generated by lifts
of its generators together with the generators of the kernel.

## Main results

* `Stacks.FiniteTypeIdealSheaf.fg_comap_of_surjective`: the ring-theoretic step — the preimage
  of a finitely generated ideal under a surjection with finitely generated kernel is finitely
  generated.
* `Stacks.FiniteTypeIdealSheaf.isFiniteType_map_of_isClosedImmersion`: the direct image of a
  finite type sheaf of ideals along a closed immersion with finite type kernel.
* `Stacks.FiniteTypeIdealSheaf.isFiniteType_comap_sup_comap`: the join of two finite type
  sheaves of ideals pulled back to a common scheme is of finite type.
* `Stacks.FiniteTypeIdealSheaf.exists_isFiniteType_le_map`: **directedness** of the finite type
  sheaves of ideals under pullback, in the shape the index poset of [Stacks, Tag 05JS]
  consumes.

## Implementation notes

Everything here lives in `Stacks.FiniteTypeIdealSheaf` rather than in
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData`, so that no name is added to the namespace Mathlib
and the rest of this tree already populate; the cost is that these results are not available
through dot notation on an `IsFiniteType` hypothesis.

No `@[stacks]` attribute appears. The sectionwise formula for the inverse image along an affine
morphism, which [Stacks, Tag 01QX] would cover, is already proved as
`Stacks.IdealSheaf.ideal_comap_of_isAffineHom`, and finite type of the inverse image along an
arbitrary morphism is already
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.IsFiniteType.comap`; the directedness below is not the
content of a tag of its own.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 05JS](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05JS)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01TV](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01TV)
-/

@[expose] public section

open CategoryTheory AlgebraicGeometry AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData

universe u

namespace Stacks.FiniteTypeIdealSheaf

/-! ### Preimages of finitely generated ideals -/

/-- The preimage of a finitely generated ideal under a surjective ring homomorphism with
finitely generated kernel is finitely generated.

Lifting the generators of `J` through the surjection gives a finitely generated `a` with
`a.map φ = J`, and `Ideal.comap_map_of_surjective` identifies `J.comap φ` with `a ⊔ ker φ`. -/
@[ega "IV 1.4.1" "more general"]
theorem fg_comap_of_surjective {R S : Type*} [CommRing R] [CommRing S] (φ : R →+* S)
    (hφ : Function.Surjective φ) (hker : (RingHom.ker φ).FG) {J : Ideal S} (hJ : J.FG) :
    (J.comap φ).FG := by
  classical
  obtain ⟨t, ht⟩ := hJ
  have hmap : (Ideal.span (Function.surjInv hφ '' (t : Set S))).map φ = J := by
    rw [Ideal.map_span, Set.image_image, ← ht]
    congr 1
    exact (Set.image_congr fun x _ ↦ Function.surjInv_eq hφ x).trans (Set.image_id' _)
  have hcomap : J.comap φ = Ideal.span (Function.surjInv hφ '' (t : Set S)) ⊔ RingHom.ker φ := by
    rw [← hmap, Ideal.comap_map_of_surjective φ hφ, RingHom.ker_eq_comap_bot]
  rw [hcomap]
  exact Submodule.FG.sup (Submodule.fg_span (t.finite_toSet.image _)) hker

/-! ### The lattice extremes -/

variable {X Y Z : Scheme.{u}}

/-- The zero sheaf of ideals is of finite type. -/
theorem isFiniteType_bot : (⊥ : X.IdealSheafData).IsFiniteType := fun _ ↦ Submodule.fg_bot

/-- The unit sheaf of ideals is of finite type: it is generated by the section `1`. -/
theorem isFiniteType_top : (⊤ : X.IdealSheafData).IsFiniteType := fun U ↦ by
  refine ⟨{1}, ?_⟩
  change Ideal.span _ = (⊤ : Ideal Γ(X, U.1))
  simp

/-! ### The direct image along a closed immersion -/

/-- **The direct image of a finite type sheaf of ideals along a closed immersion whose own
kernel is of finite type.** On an affine open `U` of the target the ideal of `I.map f` is the
preimage of the ideal of `I` on `f ⁻¹ᵁ U` under the surjection `f.app U`, whose kernel is the
ideal of `f.ker` on `U`.

The hypothesis `hf` cannot be dropped: at `I = ⊥` the conclusion is exactly that `f.ker` is of
finite type. -/
theorem isFiniteType_map_of_isClosedImmersion {I : X.IdealSheafData} (hI : I.IsFiniteType)
    (f : X ⟶ Y) [IsClosedImmersion f] (hf : f.ker.IsFiniteType) : (I.map f).IsFiniteType := by
  intro U
  rw [ideal_map_of_isAffineHom I f U]
  refine fg_comap_of_surjective (f.app U.1).hom ?_ ?_ (hI _)
  · exact f.app_surjective U.1 U.2
  · rw [← f.ker_apply U]
    exact hf U

/-! ### Directedness under pullback -/

section Join

variable (g : Z ⟶ X) (g' : Z ⟶ Y) {I : X.IdealSheafData} {I' : Y.IdealSheafData}

/-- **The join of two finite type sheaves of ideals pulled back to a common scheme is of finite
type.** No hypothesis on `g` or `g'` is needed: finite type is preserved by the inverse image
along an arbitrary morphism of schemes. -/
theorem isFiniteType_comap_sup_comap (hI : I.IsFiniteType) (hI' : I'.IsFiniteType) :
    (I.comap g ⊔ I'.comap g').IsFiniteType :=
  (hI.comap g).sup (hI'.comap g')

/-- The join dominates the first of the two sheaves of ideals, in the order given by the Galois
connection between direct and inverse image. -/
theorem le_map_comap_sup_comap_left : I ≤ (I.comap g ⊔ I'.comap g').map g :=
  (I.le_map_comap g).trans (map_mono g le_sup_left)

/-- The join dominates the second of the two sheaves of ideals. -/
theorem le_map_comap_sup_comap_right : I' ≤ (I.comap g ⊔ I'.comap g').map g' :=
  (I'.le_map_comap g').trans (map_mono g' le_sup_right)

/-- **Directedness of the finite type sheaves of ideals under pullback.** Two finite type
sheaves of ideals on schemes `X` and `Y`, together with morphisms from a common scheme `Z` to
each, are dominated by a single finite type sheaf of ideals on `Z`.

This is the directedness of the index poset of [Stacks, Tag 05JS], whose elements are a pair
consisting of an index `i` and a finite type sheaf of ideals on the vector bundle attached to
`i`: taking `g` and `g'` to be the morphisms of vector bundles induced by the transition maps
to a common later index gives the join of two such pairs. -/
theorem exists_isFiniteType_le_map (hI : I.IsFiniteType) (hI' : I'.IsFiniteType) :
    ∃ J : Z.IdealSheafData, J.IsFiniteType ∧ I ≤ J.map g ∧ I' ≤ J.map g' :=
  ⟨I.comap g ⊔ I'.comap g', isFiniteType_comap_sup_comap g g' hI hI',
    le_map_comap_sup_comap_left g g', le_map_comap_sup_comap_right g g'⟩

end Join

end Stacks.FiniteTypeIdealSheaf

end
